    id:'opqb-t24-2', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'Second vision loss in nuclear cataract is usually due to:',
    options:['Posterior subcapsular cataract','Nuclear sclerosis','Macular edema','Optic atrophy'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**The stem is malformed English and the key only makes sense once you repair it.** The bank prints **"Second vision loss"**; the sense is the *second*, later fall in vision that follows the temporary improvement of **"second sight"**. Transcribed exactly as printed and not corrected — see `source`.\n\n**The sequence the question is testing, from the deck.** `L18) Cataract II` prints it in three places: **"Nuclear: myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)"**, **"‘Second sight’: elderly regain near vision"**, and in its own self-test — *"Q: Nuclear cataract → which vision affected most? A: DISTANT vision (myopic shift). Patient may read better (‘second sight’)."* The lens nucleus sclerosing means its **refractive index rises**; `L7,8) Refractive errors` files exactly this under index myopia — **"increase refractive index of the lens nucleus (nuclear cataract)"**. The eye is driven myopic, so distance blurs while near reading briefly gets easier, and a presbyope discovers they can read without glasses again.\n\n**Why the answer is the same disease, not a new one.** Nothing stops the sclerosis. The nucleus keeps hardening and browning until it is no longer merely refracting differently but **absorbing and scattering light**, and near vision fails too. So the second loss is **progression of the nuclear sclerosis itself** — the key. `L18` puts the endpoint in its senile-cataract table: **senile nuclear, >60 y, dark nucleus on retro-illumination, hand movements when mature, phaco**.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Posterior subcapsular cataract** | A **different morphological type**, not a stage of the nuclear one. `L18` characterises PSC as an opacity at the posterior capsule sitting near the **nodal point**, so it hurts near vision and glare early and is **aggravated by miosis** — and it flags the association with **systemic steroids in a young patient**. A nuclear cataract does not turn into a PSC. |\n| **Macular edema** | A retinal cause of central loss. It can coexist, and it is why a poor result after surgery gets an OCT — but it is not what "usually" follows second sight in a lens that is still opacifying. |\n| **Optic atrophy** | A pale disc with an afferent pupillary defect. The lens is the whole story here; nothing about nuclear sclerosis kills axons, and the pupil in cataract is normal. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** "I stopped needing my reading glasses, and now I can’t see anything" is one disease told twice. The refractive half of the same fact is tested at `opqb-t9-362`, and nuclear sclerosis versus nuclear cataract at `opmcq-c9-9`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Read "second sight" as the index-myopic phase of nuclear sclerosis and attribute the subsequent visual loss to progression of that same sclerosis rather than to a new lesion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.145, Q2 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 4"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired: the stem prints "Second vision loss", which is malformed English for the second/subsequent visual loss after second sight. Supporting: `L18) Cataract II` (second sight; nuclear myopic shift; senile cataract table); `L7,8) Refractive errors` (index myopia).'
