    id:'opqb-t24-5', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Pingueculum is classified as:',
    options:['Degenerative lesion of conjunctiva','Inflammatory lesion of cornea','Vascular tumor of iris','Lens opacity'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**The classification is the whole question, and the book prints it as a banner.** `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` opens printed p.94 with the heading **"Conjunctival Degenerations"** and lists exactly two entries beneath it: **"1. Pingueculum"** and **"2. Pterygium"**. So the lesion is a **degeneration**, and it is **conjunctival** — option A on both counts.\n\n**What it is.** The book defines it as a **yellow-white subepithelial deposit of hyaline and elastic tissue adjacent to the nasal or temporal limbus, *sparing the cornea***, associated with **sun and wind exposure and ageing**, benign, sometimes slowly enlarging, and occasionally irritating through abnormal tear-film distribution. Treatment is lubricating drops for irritation and **surgical excision for cosmesis only**. `L15,16) Cornea` gives the same lesion in one line from the corneal side: *"Pinguecula: yellowish nasal/temporal conjunctival lesion; does NOT cross limbus (cf. pterygium)"*.\n\n**The discriminator worth memorising:** the book prints the mnemonic distinguishing **pterygium (grows onto the cornea)** from **pingueculum (spares the cornea)**. Both are degenerations, both are UV-driven; only one crosses the limbus. That single fact separates the two halves of the chapter’s degeneration section.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Inflammatory lesion of cornea** | Wrong tissue and wrong process. The lesion is conjunctival, and by definition it **spares the cornea**; it is a deposit of hyaline and elastic material, not an infiltrate. Chronic irritation can make it look inflamed, which is the trap. |\n| **Vascular tumor of iris** | Belongs to a different chapter entirely — the iris vascular lesion the module teaches is **rubeosis iridis**, a neovascular response to retinal ischaemia, not a limbal deposit. |\n| **Lens opacity** | Cataract. Nothing about a limbal conjunctival mound involves the lens, and a pingueculum is visible on gross inspection with a torch. |\n\n**⚠️ Spelling — and the bank is NOT the odd one out here.** The staging flagged **"Pingueculum"** as a source defect against the standard **"pinguecula"**. Checked against the material, **the course book prints "Pingueculum" too**, as its section heading and again as the caption of Fig 6-7, while its own photograph caption reads "Pinguecula" — the book is internally inconsistent and the bank is simply following the book’s heading. Recorded, not corrected. The same lesion is asked from the House book at `opmcq-c6-6`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Classify pingueculum as a conjunctival degeneration and separate it from pterygium by whether the lesion crosses the limbus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.145, Q5 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 4"). SOURCE SPELLING kept as printed ("Pingueculum") — and the course book uses the same spelling in its heading and Fig 6-7. Supporting: `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` printed pp.94–95 (Conjunctival Degenerations banner; pingueculum definition and treatment; pterygium contrast); `L15,16) Cornea` (does not cross the limbus).'
